WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 113, coming to you on June 26th, 2026.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.800
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers of Space and Science.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.680
After eight years of continuous thrust, Beppy Columbo has powered down its engines

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.640
for the final coast toward Mercury.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.900
The ESA, a Jackson Spacecraft, has switched off its solar electric propulsion system

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.720
after operating it for eight years across the inner solar system.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.140
The mission now begins its final approach sequence with critical arrival maneuvers scheduled

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.520
to start in September. The blue glow from the engines marked one of the longest continuous

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:44.580
uses of ion propulsion on a planetary mission. Engineers will rely on chemical thrusters and gravity

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.700
assists for the remaining trajectory adjustments. The next milestone watchers are tracking

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:56.040
is the September start of the arrival phase. Residual xenon propellant levels will determine whether

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.840
any extended operations are possible beyond the primary mission.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.020
Deppy Columbo's decision to shut down its ion engines marks the transition from crews to

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:11.100
arrival operations at Mercury. The solar electric system provided the gentle,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.240
continuous thrust needed to reach the innermost planet despite limited launch mass.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.080
With the engines off, the spacecraft will rely on a series of gravity assist flybys and short

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:28.940
chemical burns to enter orbit. The long propulsion phase demonstrated how electric systems can

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:35.640
stretch the reach of modest launch vehicles. What remains unclear is how much residual xenon propellant

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.480
will be left for any extended mission operations once the primary science phase begins.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.340
If the chemical rockets that lifted Beppy Columbo from Earth had to carry all the propellant needed

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:52.500
for the entire trip, the spacecraft would have been impossibly heavy. Instead, its ion engines

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:58.360
sip xenon and accelerate it to 40 kilometers per second, producing a thrust measured in million

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.840
newtons yet sustained for years. Over eight years, the cumulative velocity change reached tens of

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.240
kilometers per second while consuming only a few hundred kilograms of fuel. The journey itself

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.480
becomes a slow spiral inward. Trading time for mass efficiency in a way chemical stages cannot

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.680
match. The unanswered question is how far this gentle push can be scaled before power limits or

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.400
thruster lifetime become the next barrier. Now, shifting focus to another terrestrial world.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.160
Researchers at the University of Oxford have identified signs of enormous, earth-like

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:40.360
magnetic systems that once existed deep beneath the Martian surface. The findings challenge the long

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:46.900
held view that plate tectonics are required for such complex geology. Data from orbital instruments

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.620
revealed layered structures consistent with repeated episodes of large scale melting and intrusion.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.760
These hidden systems would have shaped the planet's crust and mantle over billions of years.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:03.180
Follow-up studies will examine whether similar features appear in other regions of Mars.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.700
On a different note, interior models for the ice giants are also being revisited.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:15.320
New interior models suggest the two outer planets could host hot, rocky magma layers rather than

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:24.060
the traditional water ammonia ice. Voyager 2 flyby data from 1986 and 1989 remain the only direct

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:30.240
measurements leaving room for alternative compositions. Revised equations of states show that

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.680
silicate mantles under high pressure and temperature could match the observed densities and

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.720
magnetic fields. The shift would change how scientists interpret heat flow and atmospheric dynamics

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:47.640
on both worlds. Future orbiter missions will test these predictions through gravity and magnetic

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:54.480
measurements. Next up, a high resolution view of our own galaxy's center. The ESA telescope

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.420
spent 26 hours collecting a high resolution image of the crowded stellar field near the Galactic

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:05.800
Center. Although this observation falls outside the mission's primary cosmology program,

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.560
the data will support gravitational micro-lensing searches planned for NASA's Roman space telescope.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:17.100
The image reveals millions of stars packed into a region only a few degrees across.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:23.060
Processing the crowded field required new algorithms to separate overlapping sources.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.420
Astronomers expect the catalog to aid studies of stellar populations and dark matter distribution

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:34.420
in the inner galaxy. Turning now to dynamics within the solar system itself,

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.900
Gaia data allowed researchers to trace a star that passed close enough to disturb the

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:46.220
ort cloud several million years ago. The gravitational perturbation is thought to have increased the

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:51.920
number of comets sent toward the inner solar system. Nathan Kaib of the Planetary Science Institute

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:59.060
and Sean Raymond of University de Bordeaux presented the work at the American Astronomical Society

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:05.520
Division on Dynamical Astronomy Meeting. Model show some of those altered orbits could still be

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.660
delivering comets today. Continued monitoring of new long period comets will help test the connection.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:18.280
Hubble has captured another early universe object in action. The galaxy observed as it

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:24.120
appeared 1.4 billion years after the Big Bang is emitting intense ultraviolet radiation,

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:30.680
capable of ionizing surrounding hydrogen. Hubble's ultraviolet sensitivity captured the object

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.660
during its brief window of activity. This detection supports the idea that small,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:41.980
starburst galaxies contributed significantly to re-ionization. The galaxy's compact size and

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:48.280
high star formation rate make it an efficient source of ionizing photons. Additional observations

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:54.280
with JWST are planned to measure its exact contribution to clearing the intergalactic medium.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.860
Commercial weather data collection is also advancing. The GEMs to Amethyst Satellite

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:07.000
launch March 30 on a space X-Transporter mission has begun returning global atmospheric data.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:12.080
Its microwave radiometer measures temperature and moisture profiles through clouds.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:17.680
Early imagery confirms the instrument is meeting performance expectations for weather forecasting

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:23.560
applications. The Boulder-based company plans to expand the constellation with additional satellites.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:28.860
Data validation against ground stations is the next step before operational use.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.700
Shifting to military communications infrastructure. The next generation,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:40.060
mobile user objective system satellites will ride on Boeing 702 MP spacecraft bus.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:45.860
The platform has already supported both commercial and wide-band military communications missions.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.860
The contract covers construction of satellites designed to provide secure voice and data links

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:59.720
for mobile forces. Integration of the 702 MP's electric propulsion and high power payload capabilities

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.420
is underway. Launch dates for the first satellites have not yet been announced.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:10.800
Rocket Lab continues its launch cadence for dedicated customers. The electron rocket is scheduled

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.520
to lift off from New Zealand today carrying the latest synthetic aperture radar satellite for the

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:22.540
Japanese company. This mission continues inspectors effort to build a constellation for all weather

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:29.020
Earth imaging. The payload will join earlier satellites already operating in orbit. Rocket Lab has

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:34.720
flown multiple dedicated missions for the customer under a multi-launch agreement. Weather and

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:40.920
range checks are the final items before lift off. NASA has selected the same provider for two

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:47.180
new science missions. Rocket Lab will provide launch services for the Polsare Ice Cloud Radiometer

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:55.900
mission and the TSI-2 solar irradiant sensor under the VADR contract. Both payloads will fly on separate

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:04.140
electron vehicles. Polsare will study polarized sub-millimeter radiation from ice clouds while TSI-2

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:10.160
continues long-term solar monitoring. The selections mark another step in NASA's use of small

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:16.300
launch providers for dedicated science missions. Mission integration schedules are now being finalized.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:22.000
Earth observation satellites are tracking current heat extremes. The satellite recorded land

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:28.760
surface temperatures on June 23 showing values well above seasonal norms in France, Spain, and Italy.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:35.220
Madrid reached 48 degrees Celsius, Poitier, 46 degrees Celsius, and Rome,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:41.740
44 degrees Celsius in the late morning. The infrared measurements provide a consistent view across

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:48.140
urban and rural areas. Copernicus services are using the data to assess heat stress impacts on

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:53.600
populations and agriculture. Additional passes are scheduled as the heat wave continues.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:09:01.840
Agricultural expansion in arid zones is visible from orbit as well. New images reveal irrigated fields

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:08.320
appearing in the desert near the Sudan border. The Copernicus satellite captured the contrast between

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:14.700
green cropland and surrounding sand. Farmers are drying on groundwater to support the expansion.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:22.000
The time series from Sentinel-2 allows monitoring of field growth and water use patterns. Similar

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:28.440
developments are being tracked in other arid regions. Astronauts and cosmonauts representing Crew-10,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:37.240
Soyuz MS-27, and Crew-11 met at Space Center Houston on June 16. They spoke about eye exams, DNA

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:43.000
sequencing of microbes, and space suit preparations from their time on the station. Ground teams were

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:48.800
thanked for supporting the research and maintenance tasks. Daily exercise routines and virtual reality

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.480
studies were described as part of the broader science program. Video recordings from the discussion

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.740
are accessible via NASA's Johnson Space Center channels. A memorandum of agreement between NASA

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:08.140
and the SBA is set for signing on June 29 at NASA headquarters. The goal is to assist small businesses

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:14.740
in competing for space-related contracts aligned with national space policy. Outreach and technical

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:20.780
assistance programs will be coordinated by the two agencies. Focus areas include reducing obstacles

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:27.880
for firms working on hardware and services tied to NASA missions. First joint activities will have

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:34.100
detailed shared following the signing ceremony. Mercury can be seen approaching Jupiter low in the

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:40.440
western sky after sunset. Its brightness continues to fade while moving past greatest elongation.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:48.980
Magnitude 1.4 is recorded for Mercury on June 26. Visibility of the pair will last several more evenings

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:54.640
until Mercury gets too near the horizon. Binoculars assist in distinguishing the planets

00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:01.160
amid the twilight. Before we go, keep an eye on the next round of Sentinel-3 passes as the European

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:07.680
Heat Wave continues. That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or review

